From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: ppc64 detect Int32 add/sub overflow with extsw+cmpd, not XER

TurboFan lowers a checked Int32 add on ppc64 to

    li r0,imm            (immediate operands only -- add has no imm form)
    addo rT,rA,r0        64-bit add, SetOE
    mcrxrx cr0           XER[OV32] -> CR0        (ISA 3.0)
    extsw rT,rT          Word32 canonical form
    bc deopt             on CR_OV32

x64 emits `addl; jo`.  On the POWER8 pool it is worse still: mcrxr is
microcoded, so ppc64le-v8-ov64-overflow-synth.patch replaces mcrxrx with
xor/xor/andc/srwi/mtcrf, giving ~11 instructions for one checked add.

The backend keeps every Word32 value sign-extended in its 64-bit register --
that is why Add32/Sub32/Mul32 each emit a trailing extsw.  Given that
invariant, the 64-bit sum of two int32 operands cannot overflow 64 bits, so
int32 overflow is exactly

    sum != extsw(sum)

which cmpd reports in CR0 without touching XER at all:

    add rtmp,rA,rB       (or addi rtmp,rA,imm -- immediate form now usable)
    extsw rT,rtmp        the extsw the canonical form needed anyway
    cmpd rT,rtmp
    bne deopt

Four instructions on both pools, against 5-6 on POWER9 and ~11 on POWER8, and
the immediate case loses its separate li.  FlagsConditionToCondition maps
kOverflow/kNotOverflow for kPPC_Add32 and kPPC_Sub32 to ne/eq accordingly.

ip is used for the untruncated sum: it is outside the allocatable set
(r3-r10, r14-r24, r30) and already serves as a scratch in this file.  Reading
both inputs before writing the output keeps the sequence correct when the
output register aliases an input.

The same identity is already used on this architecture by
ppc64le-v8-mul32-overflow-synth.patch for Maglev's Int32MultiplyWithOverflow,
which measured at parity with the native mcrxrx path on POWER9; this
generalises it to add and sub, where the immediate-form saving makes it a
strict win on both pools.

Scope: kPPC_Add32 and kPPC_Sub32 only.  kPPC_Neg32 and kPPC_Mul32 keep the XER
path so this change stays attributable; mullw already leaves the full 64-bit
product in RT, so the same treatment extends to Mul32 in a follow-up and would
supersede the POWER8 mul synthesis.  The 64-bit opcodes are untouched -- extsw
does not help there.

Found by disassembling TurboFan output from the shipped build with
--perf-prof: 3 checked adds per lin_solve iteration in Octane NavierStokes,
11 per advect iteration, 5 per am3 iteration in Octane Crypto.  Upstreamable
as-is; not ppc64le packaging specific.

